ond pair of eyes quickly on something, feel free to ask, but I can’t do the regular sessions anymore.” This leaves a tiny door open if they ever do get serious, without committing you. ### 4. If you want to be a touch more honest (but still respectful) > “I’ve noticed the applications we’ve worked on haven’t been sent off, and I’m not sure my help is actually meeting your needs right now. Maybe we should pause until you’re feeling more ready to take that step—no pressure.” This turns it into a conversation about their readiness, not a judgment on their entire community. It gives them a chance to explain, and you might be surprised. If your real reason is the hopelessness you feel about the situation (“nobody down there was ever going to use them”), you might want to sit with that privately. Is it possible you’re burnt out or disillusioned? That’s valid, but it’s a separate issue from what you say to your neighbour. Telling someone outright “I stopped because people like you never follow through” would be cruel even if it felt true in the moment. For what to say, I’d lean toward option 1 or 2—simple, clean, and impossible to reframe as an attack. If the neighbour asks why you’ve “really” stopped, you can just say, “Life’s got busy, honestly,” and change the subject. Take care with your own boundaries, but also try to leave the door ajar for kindness. Sometimes the help we give isn’t wasted, even when the paperwork gathers dust—it reminds people they’re worth investing in.
